This paper examines Bessel-type oscillatory integrals in the context of Bessel functions of the first kind, denoted as $J_{
u}$. Specifically, it establishes that for parameters satisfying $0<
u<1/2$, the trilinear operator $\mathcal{I}_{
u}$, defined by the integral $\int_{\mathbb{R}^2} f_1(x_1,x_2)\, f_2(x_1,y_1)\, f_3(x_2,y_2)\, dJ_{
u}(\lambda r) \,d\lambda \,dx_1 dy_1$, maps $L^2(\mathbb{R}^2)$ to itself.
